<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Dependency manifest for the External Dependency Manager for Unity (EDM4U).

  EDM4U resolves these at the GAME's build time through Gradle. What ships
  inside this package is this file, not the binaries.

  That is deliberate. Several vendors drop prebuilt AARs and frameworks into
  Assets/Plugins instead, and it is why their SDKs collide: our Android artifact
  brings okhttp, retrofit, gson, kotlin-stdlib, coroutines and four androidx
  libraries. Every one of those is something another vendor's SDK also uses.
  Resolved by COORDINATE, Gradle reconciles our okhttp 4.12.0 with someone
  else's 4.9.0. Bundled, both land in the project and the build dies at dex
  time with duplicate classes - and the developer who hits it has done nothing
  wrong and cannot fix it from their side.

  ANDROID ONLY since the move to Swift Package Manager (backlog D199). The iOS SDK is no longer a pod here: CocoaPods
  trunk goes read-only on 2026-12-02, so no release after that could reach a
  game through one. Editor/JoryioPostProcessBuild.cs adds the iOS SDK to the
  exported Xcode project as a Swift package (github.com/joryio/joryio-ios-sdk)
  instead, and Xcode resolves it at build time; EDM4U is not involved on iOS.
  The same reasoning holds there: Swift Package Manager reconciles the SDK's
  SQLite.swift with any other package that uses it, by package URL.

  LOCAL DEVELOPMENT, before it is published: Gradle resolves the same
  coordinate from a local source, so nothing in this file has to change.

    cd packages/sdk-android && ./gradlew :joryio:publishToMavenLocal
    then add mavenLocal() to the project's mainTemplate.gradle

  Keep the version in step with the rest of the SDKs - sdk-version-parity.spec.ts
  pins the entry below (and the iOS package version in JoryioPostProcessBuild.cs)
  with the other SDKs and fails the build if any drifts. (They sat at 1.1.0
  while every SDK was 1.3.0, because the spec did not list this file: a game
  would have asked Maven Central for a version that was never going to be
  published.)
-->
<dependencies>
  <androidPackages>
    <androidPackage spec="io.joryio:joryio-android:1.3.1-beta.1">
      <repositories>
        <repository>https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2</repository>
      </repositories>
    </androidPackage>
    <!--
      The Java bridge (Plugins/Android) compiles against Kotlin types - every
      callback it hands the SDK is a kotlin.jvm.functions.Function1 returning
      kotlin.Unit. Since 1.3.0 round 5 the SDK declares kotlin-stdlib as an
      `api` dependency (compile scope in its POM), so Gradle already puts it on
      the bridge's compile classpath. Earlier builds declared it
      `implementation` (runtime only), and the bridge did not compile
      ("cannot access Function1"). Kept here as well, at the version the SDK
      builds with, so a resolver that drops POM scopes still has it; Gradle
      resolves it up to whatever newer stdlib another plugin brings.
    -->
    <androidPackage spec="org.jetbrains.kotlin:kotlin-stdlib:1.9.20">
      <repositories>
        <repository>https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2</repository>
      </repositories>
    </androidPackage>
  </androidPackages>
</dependencies>
